&lt;p&gt;The US Federal Aviation Administration (FAA) on Friday ordered a nationwide ground stop across all American airports following a telecommunications outage that disrupted air traffic control operations across the country. The disruption affected thousands of flights, with approximately 7,000 services either delayed or cancelled, according to flight-tracking platform FlightAware.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;US Secretary of Transportation Sean Duffy confirmed the development in a post on X, saying a communication failure had affected all Air Route Traffic Control Centers. FAA technicians were investigating the issue, he added.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&quot;The FAAnews issued a nationwide ground stop for all U.S. airports following a communication issue impacting all Air Route Traffic Control Centers. FAA technicians are investigating the issue. We will provide immediate updates as we have them,&quot; Duffy stated.&lt;/p&gt;
